#~
Two inlined bodies must not share a label space (#1037).

A method's labels are numbered from its own start, so any two callees containing
the same construct carry the same numbers. The s3 inliner shifted every body by
one offset and only advanced it when the SAME method was inlined twice -- so two
DIFFERENT helpers each evaluating `->Abs()` landed on each other. The second
body's JMP resolved to the first body's LBL, which is a backward jump into
already-executed code: an infinite loop.

This test is a loop detector, so it cannot assert on a wrong answer. It either
returns or it does not. Against the previous compiler it never reaches the final
print and the suite kills it on the per-test timeout; the counter below is what
distinguishes "ran" from "ran forever" if the timeout is ever removed.

What has to be present to reproduce it, from the minimisation on the issue: two
helpers of the shape (a - b)->Abs() < x, one comparing against a parameter and
one against a literal, both called from the same method, with a float-division
expression feeding the second call. Dropping any one of those made it pass, so
all of them are kept here deliberately -- including the division, which looks
redundant and is not.
~#
class OptInlineLabelCollision {
	@ok : Bool;
	@calls : Int;

	function : Main(args : String[]) ~ Nil {
		t := OptInlineLabelCollision->New();
		t->Run();
	}

	New() {
		@ok := true;
		@calls := 0;
	}

	#~ Compares against a PARAMETER. ~#
	function : Close(a : Float, b : Float, tol : Float) ~ Bool {
		return (a - b)->Abs() < tol;
	}

	#~ Compares against a LITERAL. Same shape, so the same label numbers. ~#
	function : Near(a : Float, b : Float) ~ Bool {
		return (a - b)->Abs() < 0.000001;
	}

	method : Count(name : String, cond : Bool) ~ Nil {
		@calls += 1;
		if(cond) {
			"PASS: {$name}"->PrintLine();
		}
		else {
			"FAIL: {$name}"->PrintLine();
			@ok := false;
		};
	}

	method : Run() ~ Nil {
		p := 0.090164;

		# the first inlined body
		Count("Close against a parameter", Close(p, 0.09, 0.005));

		# a float-division expression feeding the second inlined body
		exact := (0.99 * 0.001) / (0.99 * 0.001 + 0.01 * 0.999);
		Count("Near against a literal", Near(p, exact));

		# Reaching here at all is the assertion. Two calls, not more: a backward
		# jump into the first body would run this counter up without bound.
		Count("both helpers inlined without colliding", @calls = 2);

		# the same shape again, to catch a fix that only separates the first pair
		q := 0.5;
		ratio := (1.0 * 0.25) / (1.0 * 0.25 + 0.5 * 0.75);
		Count("a third body", Close(q, 0.5, 0.001));
		Count("a fourth body", Near(q, 0.5));
		Count("a fifth body", Close(ratio, 0.4, 0.01));

		if(@calls <> 6) {
			"FAIL: expected 6 counted calls, saw {$@calls}"->PrintLine();
			@ok := false;
		};

		if(<>@ok) {
			Runtime->Exit(1);
		};

		"reached the end"->PrintLine();
	}
}
